  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{2}{5}) & =1.982\\,313\u2026\u22481.982\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.982\\,3$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.982\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.982\\,3 & \u22481.159\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.159\\,3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/250adbaae0378e3f42ab4340479bb4590aa7f39a1094ea39010de3d5758b4e3e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.159\\,3 \\\\  & \u22484.300\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\u22481.982\\,3\\cdot 4.300\\,9\u22488.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
